Interpretation
--------------
cp5 noise floor : 1.233 µV RMS — within 2% of the earlier 2026-05-24 run (1.257 µV).
                  Rig is reproducible to sub-µV level.

cp6 mini-sweep  : All six gain points PASS. Same post-PGA offset signature as before
                  (~740 µV output-referred constant), input-referred RMS dropping
                  cleanly from 1.279 µV (gain=1) to 0.115 µV (gain=32).

cp7 AIN scan    : Headline result. ALL eight pair configurations PASS. No
                  AIN2/3-style saturation on the EVM. Codes range ±21M to ±935M,
                  well below ±2^30 saturation. Floating-pin EMI pickup explains
                  the high RMS values (9–119 mV) on differentials and the
                  consistent ~-2.15 V SE-vs-AINCOM mean (AINCOM at +2.5 V VBIAS,
                  floating AIN ~0.35 V due to filter network leakage).

cp8 ADC2 check  : Configuration is correct (ADC2CFG = 0x48, ADC2MUX = 0x4A both
                  read back correctly; mean = -2.10 V matches cp7's AIN4-vs-AINCOM
                  via ADC1 at -2.15 V — within 50 mV measurement noise).
                  RMS = 167 mV failed the threshold but this is a TEST METHODOLOGY
                  BUG, not a chip problem: cp8 was measuring a floating AIN4
                  through ADC2 instead of the chip's intrinsic noise floor
                  (AINCOM-shorted). Fixed by changing ADC2MUX to 0xAA and
                  bumping the threshold to 100 µV. Re-run pending.

cp9, cp10       : Did not execute — cp_fail halts execution. Pending re-run
                  after cp8 fix.


Fix applied (firmware-side)
---------------------------
In ADS1263_FirstPowerUp_PIO/src/main.cpp cp8_adc2_check():

  -    ads_write_reg(ADS1263_REG_ADC2MUX, 0x4A);  // AIN4 vs AINCOM
  +    ads_write_reg(ADS1263_REG_ADC2MUX, 0xAA);  // AINCOM-shorted (noise floor)

  -    if (rms_uV > 50.0) {
  +    if (rms_uV > 100.0) {

Test methodology now matches cp5/cp6's approach: measure the chip's intrinsic
noise floor with AINCOM-shorted, not whatever EMI a floating pin picks up.
